    "evidence_text": "Autophagy mediates degradation of nuclear lamina\n\nAccumulating evidence supports a role for different cell cycle inhibitors in mammalian aging.\n\nA microRNA feedback loop regulates global microRNA abundance during aging.\n\nThe role of protein clearance mechanisms in organismal ageing and age-related diseases.\n\nThe ability to maintain a functional proteome, or proteostasis, declines during the ageing process.\n\nThe Aging Epigenome.\n\nExactly when and how this decline occurs is unknown.\n\nChanges in transcriptional networks and chromatin state lie at the heart of age-dependent decline.\n\nDecreased proteasomal activity causes age-related phenotypes and promotes the development of metabolic abnormalities.\n\nAn age-related decline in proteasomal activity has been implicated in various age-related pathologies.\n\nTg mice exhibited a shortened life span and developed age-related phenotypes.\n\nLamin A-dependent nuclear defects in human aging.\n\nWhether lamin A plays any role in normal aging is unknown.\n\nInhibition of this splice site reverses the nuclear defects associated with aging.\n\nThese observations implicate lamin A in physiological aging.\n\nStudies have indicated that the degeneration of hepatic function is associated with autophagic deficiency in aging liver.\n\nHowever, the underlying mechanisms still remain unclear.\n\nThis study may offer a novel insight into the mechanism involved in liver aging.",
